		{"id":"a3059b3abc5fd6c6","type":"text","text":"Ottinger\nThe article calls attention to standard setting as an important site of intervention for citizen science-based efforts to democratize science and policy.","x":-956,"y":-1140,"width":290,"height":318,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"45774625b2a1f3e5","type":"text","text":"Editor\nAdd covering note to say the most relevant person on your editorial board is ...\n\nDialogue partner get Ed to understand it properly\nIf you can hear it then it will sound right","x":-520,"y":-1111,"width":500,"height":260},
		{"id":"58915af63c1850e3","type":"text","text":"Abstract\n\n5 sentences","x":-966,"y":-1501,"width":311,"height":140},
		{"id":"160e33d783a80c82","type":"text","text":"Nold\nThis study's finding are that the legitimacy of citizen science in the legal domain rests on ==policy relevant research design and argumentation== rather than adherence to technical standards. \n\n#inquiry \n","x":669,"y":-1520,"width":311,"height":360,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"00935865117c9757","type":"text","text":"**What policy relevance means**\noften citizen sciences studies are carried out to simply define the state of the world.\n","x":540,"y":-145,"width":498,"height":165,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"4ee61ba738196bba","type":"file","file":"Airport Study Abstract.md","x":1816,"y":-2151,"width":826,"height":991},
		{"id":"b3c01c1b1e3975eb","type":"text","text":"**Message**\nThis research provides empirical evidence that the credibility of citizen-generated data derives from its policy relevance rather than conformity to technical standards. \n\nAdditionally, citizen-generated data performs differently from conventional scientific data in evoking emotional responses and facilitating expansive legal argumentation.\n","x":1058,"y":-1559,"width":560,"height":438},
		{"id":"1b7cd204f18bb252","type":"text","text":"**Introduction**","x":560,"y":-920,"width":498,"height":165,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"92156a96f71ffc9d","type":"text","text":"Focus on technical practice standards and quality in citizen science\nottinger\ncit sci literture all about quality","x":560,"y":-480,"width":498,"height":165,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"6696e4b63b221dcf","type":"text","text":"**Implications**\n\nTechnical Standards are not the main challenge or goal for future citizen science. Technical standards will always act as gatekeepers and are beyond the remit of citizen science.\n\nInstead legitimacy needs to be established tactically through more  complicated situated policy relevance. This requires collaborations take effort time and engagement with the topic\n\nThere is a danger in ideas like 'Good enough data' or data stories that offer novel forms of presentation for citizen generated data. These approaches do not require engagement validation and replication from policy opponents\n\nWhile citizen-generated data acts like postnormal science it has unique qualities such as its affective dimension that have so far not been addressed in the  ","x":730,"y":1960,"width":580,"height":680,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"5fb15f55f7a4bb90","type":"text","text":"**Credibility of citizen data is not based on technical standards but policy relevance**\n\n1 the initial data used the same technical standard but was dismissed. so it was not the technical standards or procedures being followed that made it inadmissible. The data itself and the headline finding of 1.7dB never changed. What changed was the packaging of the data the forms of argumentation, \n\n**document analysis of initial study and my paper**\n**interview with my team**\n\n2 During the inquiry there was surprisngly little discussion about technical standards of noise measurement. One of the main concern during writing the evidence statement was to discuss relative vs absolute measurements. A little in the vain of the just 'good enough data' approach the aim was to suggest a complementary or supplementary role.\n\nYet surprisingly this expected critique did not materialise. There was no direct critique. \nThis was partly because the study was replicated by the airport.\nThere was more curiosity about smart phones vs. official meters.\n**video recording**\n**my observations**\n\n3 The policy relevance of the data  in changing the proposed future mitigation of the airport they had to rush out a comparative study. This study directly addressed the cit sci study and acted as a rosette stone to enable comparison.\n**date of release of airport study**\n**document analysis**\n\n---------\n\n**Analysis** \nTechnical standards are like an aggressive bouncer that tries to stops citizen-generated data from participating. When one gets through the door the relevance means it forces replication.\n\nTechnical standards act as reliable arbiter for defining legitimacy when the specific scope of an issue has been fully framed and agreed and there is a status quo. \n\nYet in many policy contexts such as this inquiry the issue is so important and complex that there gaps for citizen- generated data to operate.\n\nIn this case the ‘systems uncertainties?’ and ?‘decision stakes meant that the citizen generated data was treated as a kind of Post-normal science\n\nissue / study area can be **reframed**. \n\nThe citizen were not just collecting data outside of the study area they were capturing a future plane - one that was currently hardly flying yet one that in the future would become meaningful. In a sense they were carrying out targeted  that was **reframing** cahng chllanging  the proposed airport mitigation measure.\n\nCitizen science data can be an act of **pre-emptive monitoring** to reframing policy problems. This only becomes apparent when one is embedded in the issue\n\n","x":600,"y":80,"width":562,"height":1737,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"32957e9f4ec48917","type":"text","text":"**Capacity of citizen data is different from normal science** \n\n**1 it is more flexible and adaptable that other kinds of evidence in the inquiry**\nthe citizen data grew bigger during the study. \nLOAEL emerged as the key issue.\nThe study finding were  not fixed and could be built on even by the laywer. \n\nWould Lois have felt as comfortable making that big contour argument if it was a formal scientific study?\n<<<<ask her!>>>>>\n\n**emails and my recollections**\n**compare opening and closing documents.**\n**evidence from interview with LOIS** \n\n2 the emotional reactions from the lawyers and the noise witness.\n\n3 Going Off script \nLois talked about there here be dragons and the airport lawyer did a verbal aside attack about its just people with smartphones that were no in their written closing statement. \n**Video transcript**\nAsk Lois why do lawyers go off script?\n\n4 Underhand critique of the study.\nThe study was not directly critiqued but done the next day by the witness in a way that is actually emotionally invovled. the laughter because launching into his defence of the methods of the airport monitoring\n\n---------\n\n**Analysis** \nCitizen science is not treated as normal science\n","x":1310,"y":80,"width":562,"height":1737,"color":"3"}
	],
